00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.130
My name is Ben Breen.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.310
I actually lead Android
Enterprise engineering,

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.850
and we're actually
based out of London.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.260
Android is actually built
between two locations.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.550
We have the largest team
here in Mountain View,

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.690
but we also have a team
of around 300 people

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:47.632
in London as well.

00:00:47.632 --> 00:00:49.090
So this session,
we're going to see

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:50.798
some of the new things
that are coming up

00:00:50.798 --> 00:00:54.630
in the next release of Android.

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.560
And I have to say
this disclaimer

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.040
that some of the things
you see here today,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.630
they may not make
it into the release.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.790
I think they will, but there's
always a chance that they

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:04.290
don't.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:14.110
So yeah, this is why
Enterprise is important to us.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.550
In 2019, Android
is forecast to ship

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:23.270
over 280 million smartphones
intended for business use.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.140
And according to
research from IDC,

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:32.420
in 2018 81% of personally
owned smartphones shipped

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.040
for businesses will
be running Android,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.290
and 67% of company-owned
smartphones

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:41.230
will be running Android as well.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:45.220
So I'm not showing these numbers
simply for vanity metrics.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.110
I'm just showing
you them to show you

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:51.210
how important
Enterprise is to Android

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.460
and why we are investing
so much time and efforts

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.100
into our B2B initiatives.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.160
So there's been sustained
investments in Enterprise

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.860
across multiple
releases of Android.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.699
In Lollipop, we introduced
something called the work

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:13.480
profile for BYOD,
which allows users

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.510
to completely separate work
and personal in their phones.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:22.810
In Marshmallow, we
introduced the concept

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:26.470
of corporate-owned,
single-use devices

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.770
and runtime permissions.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:35.410
In "nugget", so as we say in the
UK, or Nougat, as you say here,

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.760
we introduced the work
app security challenge.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.570
So getting into
certain apps, you

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:42.500
might require extra
authentication

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.580
such as a fingerprint
or pin code.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.540
And we also introduced
the digital well

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:53.050
being aspects for work profile,
allowing you to turn on and off

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.470
the work aspect of your phone.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:00.220
And in Oreo we added
zero touch enrollment,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:05.320
which means that as soon as you
take your phone out of the box,

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.380
it enrolls itself
into your organization

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.500
without the need for
any contact with IT.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:17.900
And in Pie, we introduced
multi-user improvements,

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:23.230
ephemeral users, users that
only use the device occasionally

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:28.330
or as a one off, and further
improvements to work profile.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.400
And we'll take a
look here at what

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.400
we're going to introduce
in Android Q as well.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.680
Now, most of Android
Q will be revealed

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.310
at Google I/O, which is
another conference that happens

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.530
in around one month's time.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.245
And so definitely
follow that as well,

00:03:44.245 --> 00:03:46.120
because you'll get all
sorts of announcements

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.440
around some of the
Enterprise and also

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:54.780
non-Enterprise
aspects of Android Q.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:58.950
So the Enterprise team focuses
itself on three types of user--

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.690
IT admins, ensuring that they
can easily manage and secure

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:07.710
Android devices and to minimize
the number of support tickets

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.260
that come through;
line of business,

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.110
so specialists, in-house
applications or specialist

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:19.829
hardware; employees,
ensuring that employees

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:26.700
are able to control their
privacy and digital well-being.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.980
And also, we're
ensuring that Android

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.920
is the most productive
platform for employees to use.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.040
Android also provides
multiple layers of security.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.710
So not only is it
a robust platform

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:51.240
built from the ground up to
be uncompromisingly secure,

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.920
Android also supports dedicated
security hardware, such

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.120
that you might find in
Pixel and many other phones.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.220
Android has management
APIs to allow

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:07.080
EMMs to enforce policies
and give IT visibility

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.230
into the security
posture of the device.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.780
And also there is
Google Play Protect,

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:18.040
which allows always
on app analysis

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.580
and takes automatic actions
should anything untoward

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.100
be happening on the device.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:31.140
So if we look at security
through the eyes of Gartner's

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:35.610
mobile OS security
reports, Android actually

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.930
leads the way with security.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:43.620
It's the most feature complete
in terms of built in security

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.170
and also corporate-managed
security.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:50.520
On here, light blue means weak
and dark blue means strong.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.220
And we pride ourself
in being secure.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.850
And on my team, we have a
number of Enterprise security

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:00.780
specialists complementing
the platform-wide security

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:02.270
specialists we have as well.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:09.290
So I think one of the big
misconceptions about Android

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.490
is that it's not
a secure platform.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:15.620
In fact Android security
is robust and effective,

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:17.600
offering Enterprise
grade protections

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:23.490
and controls to ensure
devices are secure.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.730
And in fact, in Android
8, there are more areas

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:30.160
which are rated stronger
than any of the competition,

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.230
according to Gartner's
reports in 2017.

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:37.290
So that includes app
updates, secure browsing,

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:41.670
authentication methods, device
wipe and corporate wipe,

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:46.430
workplace isolation, and
app monitoring and control.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:49.480
And actually, the 2019
report is due out shortly,

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:53.190
and so we'll see where Android
9 features on that as well.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.490
So Android is also
easy to deploy.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:07.610
We offer a wide range of
methods to setup devices.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:11.240
QR codes and NFC bumper
are really popular now,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.360
particularly on
dedicated devices.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.170
And in Android Oreo, we
introduced zero touch

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:20.240
enrollments, which I
mentioned before, and is now

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.240
very popular as well.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.430
And it's something you can speak
to your mobile carrier about,

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.340
and they can set
this up for you.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.490
And then on the device
management side,

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.530
we provide multiple modes
for IT admins to choose from.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:43.970
For personally
enabled devices, we

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:46.615
offer something called the
work profile, which I'll

00:07:46.615 --> 00:07:49.530
introduce on the next slide.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:53.840
So these devices are
typically referred to as BYOD.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.390
Work profile offers
a unique approach

00:07:56.390 --> 00:08:00.230
that protects work apps
and data whilst respecting

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:02.930
the privacy of users.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:08.040
And for company-owned devices
we offer three options.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.930
One of them is personally
enabled devices.

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:15.470
So we offer a work profile
for company devices

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:20.450
as well, which offers users
a separate segment where

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:24.350
they can not only
operate in a secure way,

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:28.860
but also have their
privacy respected as well.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:32.441
We also have a
work-managed mode.

00:08:32.441 --> 00:08:35.780
And for single-use
devices, we also

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:40.460
have COSU mode, where you can
lock the device to certain apps

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:41.450
for certain use cases.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.830
So this is work profile.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:51.120
This is one of my favorite
features of Android.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.620
And the way that I often
explain this is it's

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:56.930
like having two
phones in one phone.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:58.820
On the left, you've
got personal.

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:01.670
On the right you've got work.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:06.440
And actually, you can simply
turn off your work phone

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:08.210
when it's time
not to be working.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.260
So there's a switch
at the bottom.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:15.710
And there's also an
OS-enforced security boundary

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:21.350
between the two, which means
that IT can manage and do

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:23.840
whatever they want
in the work profile,

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.810
but at the same time, whatever
happens in the personal profile

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:29.600
is private to the user.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:32.270
And you can still ask
those device-wide questions

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:35.790
like is there anything untoward
happening on the device

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.860
and take action accordingly
without compromising

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:40.980
the privacy of the user.

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:48.080
So it eliminates those
scenarios where people might

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:50.210
be carrying around two phones.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:53.030
And also we've seen
one of the main reasons

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:58.760
for the lack of adoption
on BYOD as being people

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:01.520
are very conscious
about their own privacy.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.650
So this is a way where
people can maintain

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:08.970
that privacy on a single phone

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:14.310
And so some of the
benefits of work profile.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:17.550
It offers a great
work/life balance

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:20.370
it allows people to
turn off work when

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.080
it's time not to be working.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.150
But it also allows
IT to make sure

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:27.150
that the device
is secure as well,

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:29.780
and they can manage the
work side of the phone.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:38.310
Another feature that we have in
Android is managed Google Play.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:42.090
So this is a part
of the Play Store

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:46.125
which you can curate and
control as IT admins.

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:51.860
And you can also silently
push out apps as well.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.030
And if you have a
work profile, then

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:57.510
on the work profile side when
you go to the Play Store,

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.360
you will go to the corporate
part of the Google Play,

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.100
and if you're on a
personal profile,

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:06.380
then you'll access
personal Google Play.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:09.240
And you can also remotely
configure applications,

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.400
such as setting proxy
servers for Chrome

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:12.770
and all of that type of stuff.

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:20.490
Another thing we've been
working on really hard in 2018

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:23.520
is making sure that IT and
users have greater consistency

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:24.510
across Android.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:29.010
So one of the callouts that
we've had when we speak to IT

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.770
and users is the
devices can be quite

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:33.510
different from an
Enterprise context

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.520
depending on which
device you pick.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.280
And it could be quite
confusing sometimes

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:41.130
to know which device
to choose to buy.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:48.840
And so with all of
these device options,

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:52.770
we decided to try to help
standardize around some best

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:55.210
practices in the industry.

00:11:55.210 --> 00:12:00.180
And so we launched Android
Enterprise Recommended

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.130
for mobile phones.

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:04.710
And this was really
about helping

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:09.180
IT admins to make
informed selection

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.920
decisions around the phones
that they are buying.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.500
So they know if a phone is
Android Enterprise Recommended,

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:24.010
then it supports a very rich set
of features for the enterprise.

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:27.135
And it also has a high
degree of standardization

00:12:27.135 --> 00:12:28.510
when it comes to
things like UIs.

00:12:31.245 --> 00:12:33.870
Secondly, we also wanted to make
sure that all of these devices

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.260
supported zero touch enrollment.

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:43.260
And we also wanted to make
sure that all AR devices have

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:46.470
regular and frequent security
patches applied to them

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:47.680
as well.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:51.780
So by choosing an Android
Enterprise Recommended device,

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:54.720
you can be confident
that the device is

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:59.730
going to work well in your
enterprise, and consistently.

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:03.050
So, so far on the knowledge
worker side of devices,

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:08.600
we have 12 OEMs onboarded
with this program

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.320
and over 60 devices.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:15.080
And for rugged devices we have
eight OEMs and 15 devices.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:23.420
So our main focus in 2018
was launching this program.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:28.660
And our focus now is raising
awareness of this program

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:30.710
and making sure that
IT are aware and have

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:34.490
a large set of devices with
consistent capabilities

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:37.950
to choose from.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:42.210
And this year, we've also
launched AER for EMMs.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.370
So an EMM, sometimes
it's referred-- sometimes

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:45.790
people refer to them as UEMs.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:50.130
So these are your MobileIrons
or AirWatches or Google endpoint

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:54.180
management are the things
used to control and monitor

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:55.455
your fleet of devices.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:03.050
AER for EMMs ensure that
EMMs support Android

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:04.830
in the best possible way.

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:07.100
And when we launch new
features for Android,

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:10.660
they are available sooner
for you to use via your EMM.

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:17.370
So we have a number
of EMMs already

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:21.690
onboarded to this program,
as you can see here.

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:24.970
And we'll be expanding
the AER program

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:27.100
to include mobile
carriers and system

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:29.410
integrators later this year.

00:14:36.183 --> 00:14:37.975
The other focus for us
is line of business.

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:43.720
So line of business.

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:50.710
We're talking about specialist
devices and specialist

00:14:50.710 --> 00:14:55.178
apps, often built in-house
for your business.

00:14:55.178 --> 00:14:57.220
And often when we think
about Android Enterprise,

00:14:57.220 --> 00:14:59.140
we think about more
traditional use cases

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:01.990
for smartphones and tablets.

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:05.410
These devices are driving
end user productivity

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:07.480
and are used for customer
service, back office,

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:08.860
and support.

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:13.480
But where Android really shines
is for all the other device

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:16.000
types and use cases.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.590
We see single-use devices
deployed in the field

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:21.670
and set with
service technicians.

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:26.020
Customers are using
Android for rugged tablets

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.060
in harsh environments.

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:30.970
And in retail and
hospitality, customers

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:35.050
are deploying Android devices
as kiosks or points of sale

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:38.800
systems and digital signage.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:41.170
And we're starting to
see exciting use cases

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:43.240
for AR and VR here as well.

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:56.320
And because of this
device diversity,

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:59.910
we're able to serve the needs
of a wide range of industries

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:05.450
from field service to retail,
hospitality to health care.

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:07.930
And because Android
is an open platform,

00:16:07.930 --> 00:16:09.570
we, at Google, don't
need to be experts

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:12.700
in each of these industries.

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:15.810
OEM partners are able to
develop highly specialized

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:17.850
devices to meet these needs.

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:21.720
And the other exciting
part of device diversity

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:25.560
is we now have mobile devices
reaching far more people

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:26.190
than before.

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:30.247
So turning front-line
workers into--

00:16:30.247 --> 00:16:31.830
front-line workers,
warehouse workers,

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:34.155
field service technicians,
all into knowledge workers.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:41.460
And we also have
a robust platform

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:44.320
for building experiences.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:49.470
So we provide a large variety
of the leading developer tools

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:52.000
to create line of
business applications.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.460
So you have Android
Studio, and obviously,

00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:58.660
you can build in Kotlin, Java,
C++, and other languages.

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:02.970
One of the things that
I've been learning recently

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:04.020
is Flutter and Dart.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:11.010
So I tend to work on
OS, so I don't usually

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:13.660
use high-level
programming languages.

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:17.550
But I think Flutter is really
interesting because it's

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:22.980
for the first time, I
think, one of the tool kits

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:26.880
that allow you to genuinely
write code once and have

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.319
it run with the
native experience

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:30.810
across multiple OSes.

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:36.450
So you can use Flutter
to create iOS and Android

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:39.870
apps with a single code base.

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:42.940
We're using it a lot
internally inside Google now.

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:46.860
And you may have seen we also
announced Hummingbird recently,

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:51.000
which is an
extension to Flutter.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.520
I think it's in alpha.

00:17:53.520 --> 00:17:58.650
But it also allows those same
apps to run on the web as well.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:03.220
Prior to this back
in my long history,

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:06.210
I did stuff in Qt for
cross platform development

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:08.700
and then I did some
stuff in Xamarin.

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:11.310
They all had their
pros and cons,

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:13.320
but I've been really
excited about Flutter.

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:18.730
And under it as well, you
have machine learning--

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:23.380
TensorFlow Lite, and ML
Kit, so on-device ML.

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:28.180
And you have Google Play for
distribution and third party

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:31.154
development tools such
as SAP and Salesforce.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:36.610
And, of course,
developers can integrate

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:41.860
with assistance, on-device,
or Cloud Machine Learning.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:43.510
We have a few use
cases in Google

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:46.180
where we do learning on device.

00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:48.400
But we've also
got some use cases

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:50.610
where we do the
learning in the cloud,

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:53.830
but then apply the
knowledge tree to the device

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:58.840
and use the ML software
on device there.

00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:00.153
AR and VR is--

00:19:00.153 --> 00:19:01.570
Augmented Reality,
Virtual Reality

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:04.390
is becoming increasingly
important with a lot

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:09.530
of business customers and
Android things as well.

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:13.790
And of course, Android apps are
natively supported on Chrome OS

00:19:13.790 --> 00:19:14.290
as well.

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:20.900
So thinking about
employees, well, of course

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:24.920
you've got your familiar
productivity apps.

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:28.850
And the employer
can only see what

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:33.180
happens on these badged apps,
the ones with the briefcase

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.097
in work profile, if you
have a work profile enabled,

00:19:36.097 --> 00:19:38.180
which means that if you're
not using a badged app,

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:42.822
you know that you have the
privacy that you expect.

00:19:42.822 --> 00:19:44.280
And, of course,
you've got turn off

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:48.030
work mode on your work profile,
which I introduced earlier.

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:54.470
So what's next?

00:19:54.470 --> 00:20:00.110
What can we expect in Q from
an Enterprise perspective?

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:06.380
Well-- key themes, improved
end user experience,

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:10.535
more IT control, and
enhanced security tools.

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:22.750
So corporate-owned
devices can now

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:27.760
be enrolled through zero
touch, QR code, or NFC

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:30.870
directly into work profile
during setup wizard.

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:32.860
So that was previously
not possible.

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:36.610
Now if you have a
work profile enabled,

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:41.200
then you can enroll users
directly into work profile.

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:43.290
This is not for BYOD devices.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:45.100
This is for
corporate-owned devices,

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:48.130
again, giving your
users that separation

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:49.555
between work and personal.

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:58.510
Another request that
we had was on a device

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:01.150
with a work and
personal profile,

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:03.670
if I'm trying to
schedule something,

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:06.100
I need to see where
I have a free slot.

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:10.060
And I need to see both
my calendars together.

00:21:10.060 --> 00:21:12.590
And so that's something
that you couldn't do before.

00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:18.150
Now we've introduced an API,
which if your IT admin allows,

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:21.960
some calendar information can
flow between the two profiles

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.310
and can help you
find where you have

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:26.020
a free slot in your calendar.

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:28.900
And if you want to edit
something in your work calendar

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:32.020
from your personal
profile, you can do that.

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:39.070
What actually happens is the
work version of the app comes

00:21:39.070 --> 00:21:39.970
to the foreground .

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:42.400
It looks like you're
within the same app.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:44.150
You're actually not.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:46.750
It's actually switching
between the two calendar apps--

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:49.210
from the personal to the work--

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:52.060
and allowing you to edit the
details of any calendar item.

00:21:52.060 --> 00:21:57.070
So, it keeps it secure

00:21:57.070 --> 00:22:01.300
Another popular request
was separate input methods

00:22:01.300 --> 00:22:02.590
for work and personal apps.

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:06.880
So obviously, IT
have strong interest

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:08.440
to make sure that
there aren't things

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:11.890
like key loggers installed or
apps which might be sending

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:17.980
information onto another server,
whatever people are typing in.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:20.850
Well, now you can say,
for my personal apps

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:25.750
I want to use this keyboard
while still maintaining IT

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:27.880
controls for the
work apps as well,

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:30.970
so supports having two
different input methods.

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:36.850
And also more IT control.

00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:40.990
Going forward, you'll be able
to install updates manually

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:42.740
from a file.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:48.340
This allows users to test
rollouts, stage them or stagger

00:22:48.340 --> 00:22:54.860
them, and prevent bad
bandwidth or scheduling issues.

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:57.010
So you can actually
make sure that phones

00:22:57.010 --> 00:23:00.040
are getting updated when
people are typically

00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:00.970
offline or asleep.

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:06.340
And that builds on what we
added in Android 9, which

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:10.610
is the ability to freeze device
updates for up to 90 days.

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:16.880
We've also improved
Wi-Fi provisioning.

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:19.240
So IT admins can now
configure certificate

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:23.410
based Wi-Fi networks
during setup

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:31.000
and put those credentials
in a QR code or NFC tag.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:37.240
And this simplifies setup and
makes connectivity automatic.

00:23:37.240 --> 00:23:41.980
Now I think I
anticipate a question on

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:45.310
is it safe to put those
credentials into the QR code?

00:23:45.310 --> 00:23:48.242
I think it's going to be using
the easy connect standard,

00:23:48.242 --> 00:23:49.450
if you're familiar with that.

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:53.420
But I think security
shouldn't be a concern.

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:56.170
But let's see when the
final version comes out.

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:01.725
So we also support
device certificates.

00:24:01.725 --> 00:24:03.100
It was only a
short video anyway.

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:05.060
It's not a huge problem.

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:07.600
So we also support
device certificates

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:09.250
through Google Identity.

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:12.340
So now what you
can do is connect

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:17.110
to a public Wi-Fi network,
provision the device so put

00:24:17.110 --> 00:24:19.770
in your credentials.

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:22.930
And it will automatically
download the certificate

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:26.230
and then connect you to
the Wi-Fi network requiring

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:27.050
the certificate.

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:29.290
I will say simplifying
setup as well.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:34.700
Enhanced security.

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:40.145
So there is a concept of DPCs.

00:24:40.145 --> 00:24:41.520
You might not be
familiar with it

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:43.220
unless you're heavily into EMMs.

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:48.270
But a DPC is the app that EMMs--

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:52.580
like your MobileIron, or your
AirWatch, or your Intune--

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:56.790
installs onto your device
to then manage the device.

00:24:56.790 --> 00:25:02.330
Now often the EMM is
not an expert in, say,

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:07.040
analyzing network activity and
working out whether something

00:25:07.040 --> 00:25:08.890
untoward is happening there.

00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:12.140
And so what you can
now do is delegate that

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:17.570
to another app that can
do that analysis for you.

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:20.120
And the same for certificate
installation and installation

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:20.738
of apps.

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:26.870
Another common feature, which
was blocking deployments

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:31.520
of work profile, was
the ability for a user

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:35.090
to install an app
from an unknown source

00:25:35.090 --> 00:25:37.290
on their personal profile.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:39.260
Now, as an
administrator, you will

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:43.340
be able to block the
user from installing apps

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:46.670
from unknown sources on their
personal profile as well.

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:48.500
So you can guarantee
that everything

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:52.970
has come from the Play Store
and has got both the server side

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:55.556
and client side protection.

00:25:55.556 --> 00:25:58.360
So that's a feature
you'll be able to turn on

00:25:58.360 --> 00:25:59.830
as an administrator.

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:08.030
Another thing we've added is
a screen lock quality check.

00:26:08.030 --> 00:26:10.480
So we have this API
before called DA--

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:12.330
Device Admin.

00:26:12.330 --> 00:26:15.160
And that's now been deprecated.

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:19.330
One of the things that
did was allow you to--

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:24.220
or allow an app with privileges
to change the pin on a device.

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:26.620
And that was useful for
EMMs and things to make sure

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:29.590
that a strong pin was in place.

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:32.890
But it was also a door for
things like ransomware, which

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:36.340
could just put a new
password on your device

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:38.320
and ask you for money.

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:42.490
So the way we've got around
that is you can now interrogate

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:48.850
the OS to say, does the
password meet certain criteria

00:26:48.850 --> 00:26:50.410
for strong?

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:54.250
And if it doesn't, you can
promptly use it to take action.

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:57.512
Or you can take action
yourself to erase the device

00:26:57.512 --> 00:26:58.720
or do whatever else you want.

00:27:01.375 --> 00:27:04.420
We'll also be adding
private DNS supports

00:27:04.420 --> 00:27:09.010
so organizations can
require DNS over TLS

00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:13.810
to avoid leaking URL queries and
to keep all those internal host

00:27:13.810 --> 00:27:17.350
names secret and secure.

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:21.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

